BT 1 0 0 1 0 146.8 Tm /F1 9.2 Tf 13 TL (A single script is easiest for a very small program but becomes difficult to read, test and maintain. One module) Tj T* (separates the main program from utility functions and supports reuse. Three modules separate reading, statistics and) Tj T* (reporting, so each can be tested independently. A package is the clearest choice for a growing project: it groups) Tj T* (related modules under one namespace and scales well. Avoid naming a module statistics.py if it may conflict with) Tj T* (Python's standard-library statistics module; use attendance_stats.py instead. Import attendance.stats loads the) Tj T* -0.025847 Tw (submodule; importing a function directly can make the call shorter. In general, modules are loaded once and cached in) Tj T* 0 Tw (sys.modules, so later imports reuse the loaded module. Alternative concept and complexity Keeping all functions in) Tj T* (one script avoids module-import overhead and is simplest to distribute, but changes to one part can affect the whole) Tj T* -0.074323 Tw (file. A package has a small one-time import cost and uses memory for loaded module objects, but does not change the) Tj T* 0 Tw (Big-O complexity of reading or processing attendance data. Finding a function is easier when responsibilities are) Tj T* (separated and names are clear. The same function should be changed in one module rather than copied into four) Tj T* (places.) Tj T* ET
